    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d176">

                <pb facs="0159" n="149" xml:id="pg149"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/2–2449: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-02-24">February 24,
                        1949</date>.<lb/>[Received February 24—5:40 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>456. Nanking vernacular February 22 reports important meeting to be held this
                    week presided by Li Tsung-jen, who has invited Chang Chun,<anchor n="69"
                        xml:id="d176-f1"/> Hunan Governor Cheng Chien, Chang Chih-chung, Pai
                    Chung-hsi, Chung Tien-hsin and Peng Chao-hsien. Last three reported already in
                    Nanking while February 23 press prominently reports Chang Chih-chung arrival
                    Nanking.</p>
                <p>Sent Department; repeated AmEmb Canton 98, Shanghai 212.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d176-f1" n="69" xml:id="d176-f1-content">
                <p>Administrator of Political and Military Affairs for Southwest China.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
